1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:11,080
On the 26th of February, 2026, about four thousand people at one of America's best-known fintech companies found out they no longer had a job.

2
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,628
The company was Block, the business behind Square and Cash App.

3
00:00:16,048 --> 00:00:21,854
It wasn't in trouble. Its CEO, Jack Dorsey, said so himself. The business was strong.

4
00:00:22,274 --> 00:00:25,536
The reason he gave was artificial intelligence.

5
00:00:26,936 --> 00:00:31,541
Block went from more than ten thousand employees to fewer than six thousand.

6
00:00:31,961 --> 00:00:35,482
And that evening, its stock jumped more than twenty percent.

7
00:00:35,902 --> 00:00:39,093
Wall Street wasn't mourning. It was celebrating.

8
00:00:40,493 --> 00:00:47,749
If you've felt a low hum of dread every time someone says the words AI agent in a meeting, you are not alone.

9
00:00:48,169 --> 00:00:55,076
Roughly half of Americans now say they worry AI could cost them, or someone in their household, a job.

10
00:00:55,496 --> 00:00:59,459
So, is the fear justified? Or is something else going on?

11
00:00:59,879 --> 00:01:07,507
To answer that, we have to go back about two hundred years. To a group of very angry men with very large hammers.

12
00:01:16,607 --> 00:01:27,258
In 1811, in the villages around Nottingham, England, textile workers began breaking into workshops at night and smashing the machines that were replacing them.

13
00:01:27,678 --> 00:01:34,051
They called themselves Luddites. Parliament's response was to make machine-breaking punishable by death.

14
00:01:34,471 --> 00:01:41,616
In 1930, the economist John Maynard Keynes gave the fear a name: technological unemployment.

15
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:52,792
In 1964, a group of scientists and activists sent President Lyndon Johnson a memo warning that ‘cybernation’ would soon create mass joblessness.

16
00:01:53,212 --> 00:02:00,889
Every generation has had its version of this moment. And every generation, so far, has been wrong about the ending.

17
00:02:02,289 --> 00:02:09,235
Take the ATM. When cash machines spread across America, everyone assumed bank tellers were finished.

18
00:02:09,655 --> 00:02:18,291
Instead, ATMs made branches cheaper to run, banks opened more of them, and for decades the number of tellers actually grew.

19
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:28,096
Economists estimate that around sixty percent of Americans today work in jobs that didn't even exist in 1940.

20
00:02:28,516 --> 00:02:37,461
Technology destroys jobs. It also invents them. It just never asks permission, and it rarely hands the new ones to the same people.

21
00:02:42,061 --> 00:02:49,943
Here's the problem with that comforting story. The machines of the past replaced muscle. This one is going after the mind.

22
00:02:50,363 --> 00:02:56,870
Writing. Coding. Analysis. Customer support. The work a college degree was supposed to protect.

23
00:02:58,270 --> 00:03:10,782
In May 2025, Dario Amodei, CEO of the AI company Anthropic, warned that AI could wipe out half of all entry-level white-collar jobs within five years.

24
00:03:11,202 --> 00:03:15,047
And push unemployment as high as ten to twenty percent.

25
00:03:16,447 --> 00:03:23,742
This year, the CEO of ServiceNow suggested unemployment among new graduates could reach thirty percent.

26
00:03:24,162 --> 00:03:29,516
These aren't conspiracy theorists. These are the people building and selling the technology.

27
00:03:29,936 --> 00:03:32,760
Which, as we'll see, is part of the problem.

28
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:46,297
Since 2023, the outplacement firm Challenger, Gray & Christmas has tracked how many announced layoffs companies blame on AI.

29
00:03:46,717 --> 00:03:55,797
In May this year, AI was cited in forty percent of all announced job cuts in the United States. The highest share ever recorded.

30
00:03:57,197 --> 00:04:06,422
Through August, companies blamed AI for more than 116,000 cuts in 2026. Roughly one in every five.

31
00:04:06,842 --> 00:04:09,752
That sounds enormous. Until you zoom out.

32
00:04:11,152 --> 00:04:18,705
In a typical month, the US government counts somewhere around one and a half to two million layoffs and discharges.

33
00:04:19,125 --> 00:04:26,541
Against that backdrop, the AI-attributed cuts for the entire year so far add up to less than one percent.

34
00:04:27,941 --> 00:04:30,724
And it isn't even clear the companies mean it.

35
00:04:31,144 --> 00:04:37,890
Analysts have a name for it now. AI washing. Blaming the robots for cuts you wanted to make anyway.

36
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:53,043
Block had grown from around 3,700 employees in 2019 to more than 10,000. Plenty of observers said that looked less like an AI revolution and more like a pandemic hiring hangover.

37
00:04:53,463 --> 00:05:04,543
When Amazon cut 14,000 corporate jobs in October 2025, Andy Jassy later said the cuts weren't really about AI at all. They were about culture.

38
00:05:04,963 --> 00:05:17,037
Klarna, which once boasted that its AI assistant was doing the work of seven hundred customer service agents, later admitted quality had suffered, and started hiring humans again.

39
00:05:17,457 --> 00:05:24,807
The research firm Forrester now predicts that more than half of AI-attributed layoffs will be quietly reversed.

40
00:05:26,207 --> 00:05:32,734
Saying AI in a layoff announcement does two things at once. It makes the cuts sound inevitable.

41
00:05:33,154 --> 00:05:36,147
And it makes investors very, very happy.

42
00:05:40,747 --> 00:05:46,988
But if the layoff numbers are mostly noise, there's one place where the signal is harder to dismiss.

43
00:05:47,408 --> 00:05:54,693
In 2025, researchers at Stanford dug into payroll data covering millions of American workers.

44
00:05:55,113 --> 00:06:09,223
They found that workers aged twenty-two to twenty-five, in the jobs most exposed to AI, like software development and customer service, saw employment fall by about thirteen percent relative to everyone else.

45
00:06:09,643 --> 00:06:15,298
Older workers in the very same jobs? Mostly fine. Some groups were even growing.

46
00:06:15,718 --> 00:06:19,404
The authors called their paper, Canaries in the Coal Mine.

47
00:06:20,804 --> 00:06:33,802
A working paper from the US Census Bureau this year found something similar: early-career employment in the most AI-exposed industries, down around twelve percent since ChatGPT launched.

48
00:06:34,222 --> 00:06:49,193
Not everyone agrees. Economists at UCLA and the Yale Budget Lab find little sign of an AI-driven unemployment spike. They point to a broad hiring slowdown that's hitting young people everywhere, AI or not.

49
00:06:50,593 --> 00:06:58,724
But notice what both sides are describing. It isn't mass firing. It's the quiet disappearance of the first rung on the ladder.

50
00:06:59,144 --> 00:07:03,363
Nobody gets a layoff email for a job that was never posted.

51
00:07:07,963 --> 00:07:11,512
So why does the fear feel so much bigger than the data?

52
00:07:11,932 --> 00:07:22,509
Part of it is who's telling the story. The loudest voices on AI and jobs are the people raising billions of dollars on the promise that it will change everything.

53
00:07:22,929 --> 00:07:29,189
Part of it is the stock market, which has learned to reward any CEO who says the magic words.

54
00:07:29,609 --> 00:07:40,735
And part of it is that the damage, where it does exist, is invisible. A hiring freeze doesn't make headlines. A graduate sending their two-hundredth application doesn't trend.

55
00:07:42,135 --> 00:07:51,360
In March, a Quinnipiac poll found thirty percent of Americans worry their own job may become obsolete. A year earlier, it was twenty-one.

56
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:58,229
The fear is growing faster than the evidence. But that doesn't make the fear wrong. It might just be early.

57
00:07:59,629 --> 00:08:08,180
The Luddites weren't actually wrong about the machines. The machines really did take their jobs. What they got wrong was thinking they could stop them.

58
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:19,818
Their grandchildren ended up with jobs the Luddites couldn't have imagined. But the Luddites themselves didn't get those jobs. Dozens were hanged. Others were shipped off to Australia.

59
00:08:21,218 --> 00:08:28,071
That's the real question about AI. Not whether there'll be work in twenty years. There almost certainly will be.

60
00:08:28,491 --> 00:08:33,367
It's who pays for the transition. And whether anyone is planning for it at all.

61
00:08:33,787 --> 00:08:36,730
Right now, the honest answer is, not really.

